  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/26-flatfishes/euryglossa_panoides_en-2/",
      "html": "<p>Flounders and tongues belong to a group of fishes that are rarely seen in the aquarium hobby. Whereas flounders have a well developed, clearly visible caudal fin, in the tongues the dorsal, ventral and caudal fins are melted. Euryglossa panoides, which is occasionally imported from India and Vietnam, belongs to the tongues. The fish can reach a maximum size of about 20 cm, but even in the wild it rarely gets that big, staying at about half that length.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/26-flatfishes/euryglossa_panoides_en-2/",
      "html": "<p>Origin: India and Vietnam.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/26-flatfishes/euryglossa_panoides_en-2/",
      "html": "<p>It can be kept in pure freshwater, but if the total hardness is below 10° dGH, a salt addition of about 5 ‰ is highly recommended.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "equipment",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/26-flatfishes/euryglossa_panoides_en-2/",
      "html": "<p>It is very important that the fish can bury itself in fine sand, otherwise it will get skin diseases such as fungus and others.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/26-flatfishes/euryglossa_panoides_en-2/",
      "html": "<p>The most preferred food is tubifex and bloodworm.</p>"
    }
  ],
